==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[tip-toe]]
* [[tippytoe]], [[tippy-toe]]

===Etymology===
{{compound|tip|toe}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtɪp.toʊ/}})
* {{audio|En-us-tiptoe.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|ɪptəʊ}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{label|en|usually|plural}} The tips of one's toes [[collectively]].

====Derived terms====
* [[on tiptoe]]

====Translations====
{{trans-top|tips of one's toes}}
* Scottish Gaelic: {{t|gd|corra-biod|m}}
{{trans-mid}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Standing [[elevated]], on or as if on the [[tip]]s of one's [[toe]]s.
#* Shakespeare
#*: Night's candles are burnt out, and jocund day / Stands '''tiptoe''' on the misty mountain tops.
#* Byron
#*: above the '''tiptoe''' pinnacle of glory
# Moving [[carefully]], [[quietly]], [[warily]] or [[stealthily]], on or as if on the [[tip]]s of one's [[toe]]s.
#* Cowper
#*: with '''tiptoe''' step

===Verb===
{{en-verb|tipto|e|d}}

# To walk quietly with only the [[tip]]s of the [[toe]]s touching the ground. {{defdate|from late 14th c.}}

====Related terms====
* [[tiptoe around]]

